#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20b8dd4-3ddd-55b3-86fb-83375e88024f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313226} {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} {:answer/id #uuid "82692d2c-fd1e-5b01-b3cb-6c8a0df337ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313228} {:answer/id #uuid "e2935a20-1892-5012-9267-697c870c8e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313229} {:answer/id #uuid "327e9d84-65fe-5e64-abf1-9e0e1ec85e54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{2}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41cbfb8-13e7-5a6a-a618-8f4d2b734f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313227} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdadce8-c65a-5ba9-962b-2b2deff8f5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50f47d99-0d29-59c2-bb2e-982faa827891" :question/math-academy-id "q-109289" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Increasing or Decreasing Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e30da0-c514-5d6b-a4f2-32b57f53eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313233} {:answer/id #uuid "feb1851d-1cdc-5e66-b8be-5e49f929eb6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313234} {:answer/id #uuid "13ed8951-f25a-51bc-80b8-2224b159127c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313235} {:answer/id #uuid "c3741a95-9af5-5d6c-82c3-c2495ede1033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313236} {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b552bf9-1beb-5221-a168-63e371ef621b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313237} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b305db0-a24f-59f4-9972-7b09dd35b380" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2912d1ed-8c93-531d-8d23-8555cf0ff32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109290" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6ae59a-ef01-59d9-be2a-45a738702684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313240} {:answer/id #uuid "22238f90-983a-58d5-871e-8fa1a5e45d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313241} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd8d30-4646-5be8-a435-41eb7a14e022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313242} {:answer/id #uuid "583ab37d-d38a-563f-9e91-5b988ae778cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313243} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb0bd13-8b0d-5477-b3ec-c01f9997fc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63cd8d30-4646-5be8-a435-41eb7a14e022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313242} :answer-field/id #uuid "5771e197-f85c-5afa-a2e0-a3495933c4d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7704a427-55d1-5f8e-bf57-d941d8544127" :question/math-academy-id "q-109307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =14t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(14t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =14-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-6t & =0 \\\\ -6t & =-14 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{7}{3}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{7}{3}) & =14(\\frac{7}{3})-3{(\\frac{7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{3}-\\frac{49}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d3254d-3bed-51d0-8297-233436b64199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d3254d-3bed-51d0-8297-233436b64199" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313247} :answer-field/id #uuid "a079644d-0ffe-5199-814c-409514ebc282" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd9a4b56-1f5c-5ae0-ba62-c83012a38d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9a4b56-1f5c-5ae0-ba62-c83012a38d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313249} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87fac21-c636-55be-8f97-5c5744f6805a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32a4841d-90ea-5da4-90fb-13141a3fa68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335924" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-1 \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8155132e-fb38-5ac5-a729-ddd6db4a43fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313252} {:answer/id #uuid "69b7e53b-44b6-5593-8a6e-deb2504add7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313253} {:answer/id #uuid "df7e3861-6845-507f-a3fe-2ab1edef4223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313254} {:answer/id #uuid "38f0227d-9324-56e2-bff3-5e9ab523f2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313255} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b9e018a-54ab-5d05-8046-62e2477ef1c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313256} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee01c62-f6b0-5470-ac4f-67bfcd38591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f742d5d3-d314-55a4-a2e7-77b78815a252" :question/math-academy-id "q-73335" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$"}]}
